  "designNotes": "One smelter tops out at 1 plate per 6 ticks but a motor per 12 ticks needs 1 plate per 4 (2 for the gear, 1 for the rod), so two smelters are required and the ore belt must be split; the splitter is work-conserving, so the rod branch (which only needs a third of the ore) backs up and the gear branch soaks up the rest, no explicit ratio needed. The gear and rod belts must reach different assembler ports: merging them onto one belt looks like it saves a port but the faster rod press floods the belt and deadlocks the assembler at start-up. The rocks make the tight packing (direct smelter-to-press feeds, a press feeding the assembler with no belt) the cheapest, smallest and fastest build; looser layouts pass with more belts, more footprint and later first delivery.",
  "par": {
    "footprint": 70,
    "cost": 137,
    "latency": 45
  }
}
